Z||000005||theogre||07-13-2001||11:54 PM||||You do not need a new video card.<P>You need a small box that plugs in to your existing video card just like you monitor does. Most of these boxes have RCA and SVHS outputs now and work with PC and Mac. (Mac must support external monitors.)<P>The box will come with instructions telling you exactly what video modes are supported. You go into control panel/display and set the required mode. plug the box in. Press record on the VCR and start the show. (If the computer is doing the sound you need to patch that to the VCR as well.) Any VCR with RCA or SVHS input will work.<P>These boxes are available from many better computer stores, mail order, and online.<P>One such box maker is the AVerMedia. Go to Compusa.com and search for AVerMedia Try them at other computer/electronic sites as well. cnet.com is a good place to search too. <P>If you do go with a new video card... ATI All-in-Wonder cards are pretty decent. Video in out as well as decent VGA acceleration.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Songman||07-14-2001||12:27 AM||||Yeah, but can you watch TV on it?!? heheh<P>I record a lot of video in to my machine for using on websites. Plus I like to be able to have the race in a little window in the background when I am working. I had an extra slot so I got the card. Of course there is  always more than one way to skin a cat. <P>btw... I love the quality on my card...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
